{% set local_problem %}
<p>Madison sits on clay soil that doesn't absorb water worth a damn. Many neighborhoods also have high water tables, especially closer to the Tennessee River watershed. Combine that with 55+ inches of annual rainfall, and surface downspout extensions just don't cut it.</p>

<p>Here's what happens: You add a 6-foot extension to your downspout. Water exits 6 feet from your house. But on clay, that water doesn't soak in — it spreads across the surface, following the path of least resistance, often right back toward your foundation. The extension moved the water, but the clay returned it.</p>

<p>Underground drains solve this by routing water far enough that it can't return. Fifteen feet is good. Twenty-five is better. Fifty feet to the property edge? Now you're actually solving the problem.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">Madison homes</a> with persistent wet basements, foundation cracks, or standing water after storms, underground drains are often the only permanent fix. They're an investment, but compare the cost to foundation repair ($10,000-30,000) and it's not even close.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes that connect to your downspouts and carry water away from your home through a sealed, sloped channel. Water exits at a predetermined point far from your foundation — typically at the property edge, a storm drain connection, or a dry well.</p>

<p><strong>What we install:</strong> 4-inch Schedule 40 PVC pipe — smooth interior, no corrugations. Proper slope (1-2% grade) to ensure water flows out rather than pooling in the pipe. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits at the discharge point. Clean-out access points for future maintenance.</p>

<p><strong>What we don't install:</strong> Corrugated flex pipe. This is important. That black corrugated drainage pipe from Home Depot seems like a cheap alternative, but the corrugations trap sediment and debris. Roots grow into the ridges. Within 3-5 years, most corrugated pipe is partially clogged. We dig up failed corrugated systems constantly. Smooth PVC costs more upfront but actually lasts.</p>

<p><strong>The distinction:</strong> Underground gutter drains handle roof runoff. French drains handle groundwater. Different systems for different problems. Some properties need both. We'll assess what's actually going on with your water and recommend accordingly.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage throughout <a href="/recent-projects/">Madison County</a> for over 20 years. We understand the soil conditions, water table challenges, and drainage patterns specific to this area.</p>

<p>Our systems are designed to last decades with minimal maintenance. Quality materials, proper installation techniques, thoughtful design. We're solving your drainage problem permanently — not patching it for a few years.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Every spring we dig up corrugated drainage pipe that failed. Homeowners installed it themselves or hired a landscaper who didn't know better. The pipe is clogged with sediment, roots have invaded the corrugations, and water isn't flowing. They end up paying twice — once for the failed system, once for us to replace it with PVC. Do it right the first time.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If you're spending money on underground drainage, spend the extra 20-30% for smooth PVC. The corrugated stuff isn't cheaper when you account for the fact that it fails and needs replacement. This is one place where "saving money" doesn't actually save money.</p>
{% endset %}

